 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_CANCEL','Cancel'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_CLOSE','Close'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_PREVIOUS','Previous city'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_NEXT','Next city'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_GREETING_1','You stand within Genesis Spire. How many worlds have your people altered, and by whose authority?'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_WAR_1','You claim the right to shape these worlds. We will see what remains of that claim in the bodies of your descendants.'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_ATTACKED_1','Your weapons can end a life. We have spent ages deciding what may follow it.'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_DEFEAT_1','Close the vaults. Whatever survives here will have a future beyond our design.'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_PEACE_1','Your people may keep their present form. We will keep the terms of the truce.'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_FALLBACK_1','Speak plainly. What do your people wish us to leave as it is?'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_CIV5_HEADING_1','The Qu in All Tomorrows'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_CIV5_TEXT_1','In C. M. Kösemen’s All Tomorrows, the Qu are an alien species nearly a billion years old. They migrate between the arms of the galaxy, altering their own bodies over the course of their travels. Their grasp of genetics and nanotechnology gives them the means to reshape entire worlds. A doctrine once meant to restrain their power hardens into a religious conviction that they alone have the right to determine what life will become.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]When the Qu encounter the Star People, humanity’s descendants have already terraformed many worlds. The Qu regard this as a challenge to their mandate. They shatter the human civilizations in less than a thousand years and remake many survivors into animals, living tools, or experimental forms. After forty million years of rule, the Qu depart. Some altered human lineages survive and begin to evolve beyond the purposes their conquerors assigned them.'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_CIV5_HEADING_2','Playing the Qu'),
 ('TXT_KEY_CWC3_QU_CIV5_TEXT_2','The First Architect, Genesis Spire, and the three castes are inventions for this mod. Divine Reconfiguration adapts the Qu’s practice of remaking conquered populations into a city ability.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Gain 1 Biomass from eligible enemy military kills (up to 3 per turn) and 2 when entering a new era. Store up to 10 Biomass. Spend 5 Biomass and 1 Population to install one genetic caste in an eligible annexed conquest. Open Genetic Reconfiguration with the Biomass button or from Additional Information.'),
